      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 1B.1 — Rational Function Asymptotes, Removable Holes & Oblique Quotients",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>For a rational function \\( r(x) = \\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \\): if a factor \\( (x - c)^m \\) appears in both \\( P \\) and \\( Q \\) with at least as high multiplicity in \\( P \\), then \\( x = c \\) is a <strong>removable discontinuity (hole)</strong>; otherwise \\( x = c \\) is a <strong>vertical asymptote</strong>. When \\( \\deg(P) = \\deg(Q) + 1 \\), polynomial division \\( P(x) = (mx + b)Q(x) + R(x) \\) yields the <strong>slant (oblique) asymptote</strong> \\( y = mx + b \\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
